    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Ontology categories: Subject is the exact thing a claim, representation or transformation concerns, identified by inventory and bytes or semantic addresses. Representation is bytes, text, model or program standing for a subject. Interpretation is a declared context-bound representation-to-meaning contract identifying reliable distinctions and residuals. Context declares subject, definitions, basis and admissible observations. Outcome U is an owner-selected directly verifiable state with acceptance interval. Object O has scoped identity (authority, kind, value), boundary, state and material relations. Actor performs a step within context, attention, time and capability limits; role is an occupied relation selected by configuration. Step is bounded intake, selected context, grant, execution and output. Step-produced fact is a judgment, residual, observation or authority decision that cannot be reacquired from declared sources; reproducible observations are excluded. Claim binds subject, property, basis, witness relation and falsifier. Evidence comprises admitted exact observations/artifacts with provenance, subject identity, basis and scope. Judgment is recorded evaluation over unchanged subject, with evaluator, decision, evidence, scope and revising observation. Authority is identity-bearing scoped permission to operate or dispose acceptance. Residual is material uncertainty, omission, loss or unproven relation awaiting authorised disposition. Basis is every exact version, identity, time, topology, authority, configuration or evidence coordinate whose change can alter the bound step, claim, result or evaluation. Materiality is evaluation-relative potential to change result, basis validity, admissible evidence, operation performer or decision owner, including uncertainty and closure.",
